<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">January <a href="/years/1856">1856</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau&quot; (&quot;The Old Land of My Fathers&quot;, usually rendered &quot;Land of My Fathers&quot;) was written in January 1856 by Evan James, with a tune by his son James James, both of Pontypridd in Glamorgan; the earliest written copy survives in the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>When it was composed the song was known as Glan Rhondda, Banks of the Rhondda. It was first performed in the vestry of the original Capel Tabor in Maesteg, in January or February 1856, by Elizabeth John of Pontypridd, and it soon became popular in the locality. The composer James James was a harpist who played for dancing in the public house he ran. The tune was meant to be in six-eight time but was slowed to its present three-four tempo when large crowds began to sing it.</p><p>Its popularity grew after the Llangollen Eisteddfod of 1858, where Thomas Llewelyn of Aberdare won a competition for an unpublished collection of Welsh airs that included Glan Rhondda. The adjudicator, John Owen, known by the bardic name Owain Alaw, asked to include it in his Gems of Welsh melody (1860–1864). That volume gave it the title Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau and sold in large numbers, which spread the song across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>At the Bangor Eisteddfod of 1874 the song was sung by the soloist Robert Rees, known as Eos Morlais. It was increasingly sung at patriotic gatherings and gradually became a national anthem. On 11 March 1899 Madge Breese sang it for the Gramophone Company in the first recording made in the Welsh language.</p><p>In 1905 the Welsh rugby team hosted the first touring team from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, which began every match with a haka. In reply the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> player Teddy Morgan led the crowd in singing the anthem, which is said to have been the first national anthem sung at the start of a sporting event. Over the next four decades it became the more popular anthem in Wales, although the official anthem was then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>. It was sung with other anthems before sporting events until 1975, when officials decided it should be sung alone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Like the other anthems of the United Kingdom, it has no official status in law, but it is used at government ceremonies, including the opening of the Senedd, and at receptions of the British monarchy since the 1970s. Usually only the first stanza and chorus are sung, in Welsh. In 1978 Geraint Jarman a'r Cynganeddwyr recorded a version with electric guitars for an album of the same name, inspired by Jimi Hendrix's version of the Star-Spangled Banner.</p><p>The tune has been adapted for the anthems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, Brittany and Y Wladfa, the Welsh settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patagonia">Patagonia</a>, where Lewis Evans wrote Gwlad Newydd y Cymry. The refrain's phrase Pleidiol wyf i'm gwlad, I am devoted to my country, was printed on the edge of pound coins minted in 1985, 1990, 1995 and 2000, and appears in the Royal Badge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> adopted in 2008.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
